    "summary": "Intense Dark Star, tight Jerry‑Phil interplay, raw early ’69 energy",
    "blurb": "The show delivers a ferocious Dark Star and a soaring The Eleven, highlighted by crisp Jerry‑Phil chemistry. Cryptical Envelopment suite unfolds with driving intensity, while the second set sustains the raw early‑’69 momentum.",
    "review": "Philadelphia’s Electric Factory on February 14, 1969 captures the Dead at a pivotal moment of unfiltered power. From the opening “Good Morning Little School Girl” the band rockets into an incendiary Dark Star that feels both exploratory and tightly locked in, with Jerry Garcia’s tone soaring over Phil Lesh’s Lovelight‑style bass foundation. The momentum builds through Saint Stephen into a mesmerizing The Eleven, where the round motif showcases the telepathic Jerry‑Phil interplay praised by listeners. The second set is a showcase of the early “Cryptical” suite—Cryptical Envelopment, a relentless drum duet, The Other One and its reprise—all delivered with raw intensity despite a slightly muffled tape. Even with cassette‑tape hiss, the recording preserves every channel, making this performance a must‑hear snapshot of the band’s pre‑Wall of Sound era.",
